WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.303 --> 00:00:08.220
ANDREA RUSSELL: I'll be
joined in a little bit

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.678
by a few customers,
and we're going

00:00:09.678 --> 00:00:13.170
to talk today about how you
can build top notch marketing

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.970
technology at scale
with Google Cloud.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.470
But, before we get
into the content,

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.600
I'd love to get a sense
of who's in the room.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.190
So raise your hand if
you're a developer.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.635
OK, there are a few of us.

00:00:23.635 --> 00:00:26.010
Raise your hand if you're in
sales or in another business

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.150
role.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.400
OK, a little fewer.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.900
Raise your hand if you work at
an advertising or a marketing

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.190
technology company.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.870
If you're already in this space.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:35.960
Awesome.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:36.460
Great.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.440
So it's nice to have
all of you in the room.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.350
And before we dive
into the content,

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:43.630
I want to start by level
setting a little bit.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.930
Over the past 10 years, there's
been an explosion of new ad

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.730
tech and martech companies.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.640
chiefmartec.com hosts
an annual conference

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.500
where they talk
about the industry

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.290
and they document the
number of companies in it.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.210
In 2016 when they
published this infographic,

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.450
there are about 3,500 companies.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.390
They just released the newest
infographic a few days ago

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.220
with well over 7,000 companies.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.720
So the growth of
technology in this space

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.600
has been supported
by CMOs themselves

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.580
who are signaling
with their wallets.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.660
A 2018 Gartner CMO study
found that CMOs are now

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.240
spending more on technology
than IT departments.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.950
And about a third
of marketing budgets

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.440
are now spent completely
on technology.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.780
But even as the
industry grows, we're

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.450
starting to see much more
mergers and acquisitions.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.640
The industry is both
growing and consolidating.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.960
And the reality is, even
as the industry sustains

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.990
enormous growth, new
challenges are emerging.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.490
For the majority
of my career, I was

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.530
working in the
advertising technology

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.010
space at a few different
companies before Google.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.670
And I've had the
opportunities to speak

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.580
to CMOs at large
brands and advertisers,

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.990
as well as CTOs and CEOs at
marketing technology companies.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.450
For the past two years, I've
been focused on our Cloud

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.910
Marketing initiative,
helping companies migrate

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.948
and re-platform to Google Cloud.

00:02:02.948 --> 00:02:04.990
And what I've been hearing
from these businesses,

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.300
these companies, is
that the way marketers

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.725
do business is changing.

00:02:08.725 --> 00:02:10.600
And I want to highlight
two challenges, which

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.898
are driving this shift.

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:14.440
The first challenge
is that marketers

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.900
are shifting from
traditional advertising

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.180
to a focus on a
customer experience.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.250
The ask has now become,
how do you create

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.950
a compelling, personalized
experience, which

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.490
drives revenue, while
also adhering to security

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.710
and privacy
Regulations marketers

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.330
and CMOs see where
the ball is going.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.820
And according to a 2017 Gartner
survey, 81% of marketers

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.640
expect to be competing
mostly or completely

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.540
on the basis of customer
experience in two years time.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.490
And in two years
time, that's now.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.330
So enterprises need to be
more thoughtful about how

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:50.900
they're managing customer data.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.910
This means new
processes, new methods,

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.810
new ways of tackling
these challenges.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.570
And in this new world
of customer experience,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:02:59.740
it's really high stakes.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.830
Because the risk is that
if you get this wrong,

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:04.220
you degrade user trust.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.880
Whether your user is a publisher
or a user at the other end

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.210
of a computer screen.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.180
The second challenge
in this ecosystem

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.890
is that differentiation
is becoming

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.320
increasingly difficult. Why?

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.420
This is actually caused by what
I would say two main reasons.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.393
The first is just the crowded
nature of the ecosystem.

00:03:22.393 --> 00:03:23.810
There are more and
more companies,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.020
we saw in that first
slide the infographic,

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.900
competing and specializing
in the same space.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.390
As competition and
scale increases,

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.780
margins start to get
compressed and companies start

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.910
to have to do more with less.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:43.190
At the same time, the speed
of innovation is increasing.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:45.500
As the world and technology
becomes more efficient,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.050
advertising becomes more
efficient, and more and more

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.800
competitors migrate
to the cloud.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.400
They become more agile,
better able to respond

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.080
to business needs.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.690
According to a 2013
study by Seismic,

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.940
marketers actually
show a clear preference

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.630
for working with fewer
vendors in this space, which

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.240
means it's more important
than ever to stand out.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:10.110
And you need a Cloud platform
that can help you do that.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.960
So responding to this
changing ecosystem

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.450
requires a change in
mindset and a different way

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.660
of thinking about how you
build your technology.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.640
Companies are just like humans.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.560
Companies are made up of humans.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.310
We like to do things
that are comfortable.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.620
Things that are easy.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:27.290
And most of the time that means
maintaining the status quo.

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.900
Doing things the same way
you did it previously.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.930
But maintaining the status quo
isn't a recipe for success.

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:34.970
And taking a new
approach often requires

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.550
making real change that
will enable you to actually

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.990
grow and differentiate
your business.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.000
Google Cloud is a
technology that's

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.640
been built to help
businesses transform.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.250
And it's actually just
an external version

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:50.380
of the technology
and capabilities

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:52.150
that Google has
built over the years

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.090
to support both our advertising
and our consumer-facing

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.110
products.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.510
You can think of Google Cloud
like the invisible support

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.960
beams that helped to make all
the cool things that Google

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.370
does possible.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.203
It's a safe, reliable, and
scalable place for companies

00:05:06.203 --> 00:05:07.120
to build and innovate.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.090
Whether you're moving an
entire marketing platform,

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.460
marketing technology
business, or just

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:15.860
migrating one small data
and analytics workload.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.910
It's also a platform that has
close ties to our advertising

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:19.750
business.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.570
And this has huge benefits
for companies in the marketing

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.640
technology space because
you can decide how

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.020
you want to partner with us.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.810
Whether that's as
an integration layer

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.530
or whether that's with a
joint go to market plan.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.580
In fact, we've already worked
with a number of companies

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.890
in this space and we've
seen great success.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.400
Mixpanel is actually a San
Francisco-based analytics

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.150
company.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.470
They track user interactions
over web and app

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.930
and they're focused on
driving and measuring

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.340
user engagement and retention.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.700
They are an analytics
company and they

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.640
had hugely demanding workloads.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.780
Their average customer query was
scanning about 50,000 events.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.820
And some of their queries
could reach as much as 10

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.710
billion events.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.790
The problem is, as they scaled,
they ran into huge issues

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:10.140
with their bare metal servers.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.230
They weren't able to reach
the same performance.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:19.200
They were running into server
downtime and system failures.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.780
So they made the decision
to migrate to Google Cloud

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.820
and they were able to
achieve higher performance

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.862
and achieve the scale that
they needed to support

00:06:25.862 --> 00:06:27.120
their enterprise customers.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.090
They wanted to
maintain a median query

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.270
time of one second
for each query

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.717
in order to maintain
that real time analytics.

00:06:35.717 --> 00:06:37.800
And I think the interesting
part about this story,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.780
it's not on this slide,
but they actually

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:43.290
were able to reclaim 30%
to 50% of engineering time

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.867
for engineering product
development, which is huge

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:48.450
when you're thinking about ways
that you can innovate and grow

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:49.260
your business.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:55.060
Reclaiming 50% of
engineering time is amazing.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.630
So companies like
Mixpanel see huge benefits

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.690
from partnering
with Google in ways

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.390
like achieving global scale
while reducing infrastructure

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.080
costs.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.370
They're able to analyze
valuable data and drive growth.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.930
And also future-proof their
business with our smart,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.018
secure, and open platform.

00:07:12.018 --> 00:07:13.935
And I want to dive into
a few of these topics.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.870
So I want to speak a little
bit about our purpose-built

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.190
infrastructure.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.650
Google Cloud has the most
extensive cloud network

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:23.790
with the farthest reach.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:25.620
We have over 100
points of presence.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.260
If you've been to our keynote
or been to any other sessions,

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.570
I'm sure you've heard
a lot about this.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.110
This means that we have higher
availability, lower latency,

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.172
and better security.

00:07:35.172 --> 00:07:36.630
And especially when
you're thinking

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:38.670
about reaching the right
user at the right time

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:43.050
with the right message, it's
key to maintain that scale,

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.600
while being secure
at the same time.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.690
And our network can
definitely help you do that.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.160
Aside from our
network products, we

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.170
have an entire suite
of data products

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.842
that are tailor-made for
whatever challenges you face.

00:07:54.842 --> 00:07:56.550
Whether that's needing
to query petabytes

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.970
of data or ingested
millions of rows per second.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.060
The cool thing here is
that, as a Cloud customer,

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.850
you have direct access
to the software systems

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.250
that we've built for
our ads business.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.880
And many of these products were
BigQuery, Dataproc, Spanner,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.060
Pub/Sub, Bigtable,
all can manage

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.790
the scale of our ads products.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.720
And you can use them
to the open standards

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.460
with proprietary performance.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.440
These products also
make it easy for you

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.360
to share your data with
your customers, which

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.270
is really important with
your customer experience

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:32.220
head on if you're thinking about
helping your customers get all

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.860
their data in the same place.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.169
I want to a dive a
bit into Bigtable.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:41.270
Raise your hand if you're
familiar with Bigtable?

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:42.380
OK, good.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.000
So if you're not
familiar, Bigtable

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.900
is a fast, globally distributed,
wide column, NoSQL database.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.570
And if all of that sounds like
gibberish to you, that's fine.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:54.110
Basically this means that
Bigtable is a super low latency

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.210
database that serves
you the information

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.530
exactly when you
need it globally.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.800
And so when you were thinking
about ad tech companies,

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.680
martech companies who we need
to serve up in real time,

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.950
Bigtable is a product that's
super, super helpful for that.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.740
And it's also fully managed.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.690
This means your
engineers can focus

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.970
on building new features,
new tools, new products,

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:17.480
rather than maintaining a
database, like BigQuery.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.630
Lastly, Bigtable
provides easy integration

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.400
with open source big data tools.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:25.530
So it's completely compatible
with existing Apache HBase

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:30.670
setups and other Apache
services, like Spark and Storm.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:34.420
Keeping on that theme, the
entire Google Cloud Platform

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.460
is built to support your
business and the way

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:39.370
that you work, whatever
your Cloud capabilities are

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.630
or wherever you are
on your journey.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.840
So over the past
10 years, Google

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:46.690
has been super intentional
in our support of open source

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.740
projects, from early
work in containers,

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.638
to Linux, to GitHub, to Python.

00:09:52.638 --> 00:09:54.430
So this means when you
choose Google Cloud,

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:55.463
you're not locked in.

00:09:55.463 --> 00:09:57.130
And if you saw our
keynote this morning,

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:59.890
you heard a lot about that with
some of our new announcements.

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:01.570
But basically, we
want to help you

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.900
do what you want to do and use
our products in the ways that

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:06.950
supports your business best.

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.820
So you can use GKE you on
prem to create a single pane

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.330
view for managing
all your clusters,

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:14.630
regardless of where they are.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.390
And this leaves
you free to migrate

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.550
what makes sense for
you, but also gives you

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.130
flexibility and control.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.670
So, to summarize, Google Cloud
is built from the bottom up

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.370
to help you build smarter
and innovate faster.

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:31.250
Whether that's providing a
unique customer experience

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:34.430
or analyzing data to
help your customers

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.200
receive richer insights.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.030
It's a powerful
platform that's built

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:41.480
to help you build a better
version of your business.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.220
And I want to bring
up a few customers

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.340
to share about how they're
using Google Cloud, why

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.520
they choose Google Cloud, and
what they're building next.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:53.530
So I'd love to invite up
Paul Ryan, the CTO of OpenX.

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:54.838
Put your hands together.

00:10:54.838 --> 00:10:58.822
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:00.437 --> 00:11:01.312
PAUL RYAN: Thank you.

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:06.470
Hello.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:07.940
I'm Paul.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:10.790
So what is OpenX?

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:13.130
OpenX is an ad exchange.

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:14.730
What does that mean?

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:18.440
That means we get around
2.5 trillion requests

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:22.220
for ads a month, which
translate into 4.5

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:26.480
trillion specific events
per day that we track.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.820
We work with publishers,
the supply side,

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.430
and help them fill
their inventory slots.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.400
We reach out to the
demand side, a couple

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.060
of hundred folks that are
proxies for advertisers.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.530
And so it's a big exchange.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:42.800
High volume
transaction processing.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.370
Lots of things going on.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.580
Lots of moving parts.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:51.170
OpenX is 100% on prem right now.

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:52.970
And we're migrating into GCP.

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:55.340
As we speak, we're
testing in Singapore

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:58.730
for what we call our delivery
sites, our front end exchange

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:02.910
site, which actually
runs the auction.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.660
So why would a
company this large

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:09.680
with such demanding online
transaction processing

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.140
requirements move to the cloud?

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:14.105
Isn't the cloud like
way more expensive,

00:12:14.105 --> 00:12:15.230
and it's not going to work.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:18.390
And how is it going to scale
to the workloads that you need?

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.120
So we had all these
questions pretty much

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.640
at the beginning of 2018.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.090
And so we went on a journey
to figure out specifically

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.340
how the public
cloud might help us,

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.470
potentially in a hybrid mode.

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:31.970
With a little bit
of investigation

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:34.687
we said, well, what the
hell, why go hybrid?

00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:36.770
Why don't we see what it
looks like if we're fully

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.697
in the public cloud?

00:12:38.697 --> 00:12:41.030
And so I'm going to talk a
little bit about that journey

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.910
and give you a little bit
more background on OpenX

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.850
and why we decided to use GCP.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.490
So on the publisher side,
we work with basically

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.680
all the largest
publishers in the world.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.740
People that really want to
fulfill their ad requirements

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.540
and actually generate revenue.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.570
So, obviously, it is a
very demanding client set.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:01.310
And there are some
unique challenges

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:03.500
working with both the
publishers and the demand

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.810
side of the ad tech ecosystem.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:11.240
And, frankly, if you look at
the LUMAscape or the chart

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.880
that we just saw, there's
thousands of players.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:16.880
But, fundamentally,
when a user is on an app

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.550
or on a publisher's site,
potentially, they're

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.980
working through a
JavaScript container

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:25.790
to call out to lots
of different exchanges

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:28.430
that each call out to a lot
of different demand side

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.140
platforms, which
are representing

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:32.370
brands for advertising.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:36.770
So this diagram tells you
kind of the problem set.

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:39.920
There's a tremendous amount
of networking going on.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.160
There's a tremendous amount
of requests going out.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:44.900
And then you've got
to process them, save

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.140
all the requests and the
responses, to figure out, oh,

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:49.670
who won the auction?

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:51.240
Did you get an impression?

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.090
And who do you pay the money to?

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.710
And who do you bill?

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.930
So that's kind of the ecosystem.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:57.920
There's tons of tech.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:00.470
There's lots of different
companies involved.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:03.380
And actually, EB is
Exchange Bidding.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:04.885
That's Google.

00:14:04.885 --> 00:14:06.260
And so Google is
heavily involved

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:08.580
in the programmatic ecosystem.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.660
So what's really needed to win
in programmatic is you have

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:13.280
to have a global footprint.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:17.150
Even 100% on prem, we have
five data centers worldwide.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:20.420
Because you have to be close
to the supply side, the client,

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:23.450
the consumer, the
person using the app.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:25.590
That latency is very important.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:28.310
But you also have to be
close to the demand side

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:30.720
because you're requesting
bids from them.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.780
And so you kind of
have to place things

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.730
in the middle around
the world in order

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:37.430
to get the best performance.

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:39.290
You want the lowest
latency, as I said,

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:42.170
to the endpoints and
consumers that you're using.

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:44.940
And frankly, it's
got to be flexible.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:47.690
I mean, if our average
CPU utilization on prem

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:51.450
is like 20%, that's all
you need to know, right?

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.320
It's a 5x trough
to peak universe.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.540
And gosh, isn't
that auto scaling?

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:59.300
Can't you do
something about that?

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.780
And it's really real-time
and I call web scale.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:06.590
So at any given moment, we could
be up to 2 million requests

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:08.190
per second.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.860
And as I said, more
than $4 trillion events.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.710
So this is a pretty
demanding set of workloads.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:16.130
So how do you select a
public cloud provider?

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:18.200
There's only three
easy steps, right?

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:20.490
Everything's three easy steps.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:25.080
So you identify the providers
that have a worldwide footprint

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:28.620
and can operate at that scale.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.260
And realistically, there's
probably three or two

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.186
and a half.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:37.250
Maybe two and three quarters.

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:39.590
So you identify
the usual suspects

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:41.810
that you would see in
the Gartner quadrant

00:15:41.810 --> 00:15:43.850
up to the right.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:46.310
Then you actually have
to do a proof of concept.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:49.340
Because your mileage will vary.

00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:51.080
Depending on the
critical components

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:54.800
of your infrastructure, they're
going to behave differently.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:58.660
Regardless of whether you're
using Kubernetes on parameter

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:01.860
100% containerized, it's going
to be a little bit different.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:05.000
The bang for the buck in
instances will be different.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.050
The networking profile
will be different.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.110
So you've got to POC some stuff.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:10.580
And then you model it.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.040
You figure out if
it's going to work.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.110
You work with the
CFO to convince them

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:19.340
that not having capex
is a good thing.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:20.840
Because all of the
sudden, your COGS

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.480
go up because it's all variable.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.250
And so you have to
have that discussion.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:28.670
On a cash flow basis,
it might be fantastic.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.330
But from an EBITDA point
of view, it may not be.

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:33.080
So you have to have
all those discussions,

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.930
model it, and
actually negotiate.

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:41.840
So we did a whole series of POCs
with the three usual suspects

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:44.870
in terms of our front
end, which is, as I said,

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.790
running the auction.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:50.270
So we took four or five
of our major components

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:52.220
and ran them in all
the public clouds.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.880
And then peered
directly with our colos

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:59.150
for all the other services,
for the 150 other services

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:00.230
that we use.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:04.280
And ran real production
loads against it,

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:08.690
changing up sizes and figuring
out how we would potentially

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:10.400
scale stuff.

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:14.560
And there's some relatively
meaningless graphs up there.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:15.944
Don't read anything into it.

00:17:15.944 --> 00:17:17.569
But we actually
performance checked it.

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:20.810
Ran it up to around
1,000 QPS just

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:23.599
to see how the
different profiles work.

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:30.390
On the back, our
hourly ETL process,

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:34.940
which is really this enormous
set of MapReduce jobs

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:37.130
with some Spark stuff in there.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:39.830
We took that and said,
OK, here's an hour,

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:43.800
here's two hours, here's three
hours of raw transaction data.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:48.920
Let's see how much we can scale
things like EMR or Dataproc

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.230
to see if we can actually
do that workload in less

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:52.700
than an hour.

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:56.900
So these were the
two major components.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:18:01.110
Can we run an auction and can
we actually process the data?

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.430
So what have we
discovered about GCP?

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:10.470
Well, the network latency in GCP
in general is just outstanding.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:14.010
To everyone in the programmatic
ecosystem, to Google,

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:15.460
of course.

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:18.320
And why should anyone
find that surprising?

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:20.720
If you're in
programmatic advertising,

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.000
is it in your interest
to be peered very, very

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:25.610
well with Google and GCP?

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:27.400
Of course.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:28.930
So if you're in
that infrastructure,

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.030
your latency is fantastic.

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.785
You get pretty good bang
for the buck on the VMs.

00:18:33.785 --> 00:18:36.160
And we could do this across
the different cloud platforms

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.830
to figure out, for a
given workload, what

00:18:38.830 --> 00:18:41.912
do we need to do.

00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:43.370
The other thing
is, remember I said

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:45.320
we're doing this
periodic hourly thing,

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:49.410
where I might need 30,000 to
50,000 cores to do something.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:52.130
Well, you know, if it
takes somebody two weeks

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:54.680
to try to get that test
to run, that kind of

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:58.220
is an indication they don't have
enough resources to actually

00:18:58.220 --> 00:18:59.340
run the business.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.240
So that was a availability
of large resources.

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:04.400
And frankly, the
open source support

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:08.160
with things like Kubernetes,
it's just it's undeniable.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.290
So that's kind of why
we chose Google Cloud.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:10.790
Thank you.

00:19:13.718 --> 00:19:16.220
ANNEKA GUPTA: Well, thank you
for joining for the session.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:20.620
We at LiveRamp ramp had a
very similar story to OpenX

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:23.530
in terms of the process
that we went through

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:25.960
in looking at Cloud
solutions, the decision

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:31.410
to move to the cloud, and
our decision to use Google.

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:35.130
So LiveRamp's business,
the way we operate,

00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:38.660
we're really this data
and identity foundation

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:40.310
for marketers.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.320
You heard Andrea
talk about the fact

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.900
that today marketers are
focused on how do they

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:47.540
build incredible
customer experiences.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:49.040
And, in order to
do that, they need

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.938
to be able to harness
all of their data

00:19:50.938 --> 00:19:52.730
across all of the
interactions that they're

00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:55.520
having with their customers
and be able to leverage

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.380
that data to make
better decisions

00:19:57.380 --> 00:20:00.390
and interact with our customers
in more meaningful ways.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:03.140
So we're a key foundational
middleware layer

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:06.770
that enables brands to bring
together all of the data

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:08.990
that they know about
their customers,

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:11.210
segment it, create
audiences, and then

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.580
activate it across
any range of ad tech

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:16.760
and martech technologies.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:21.890
So to give you some history
about where LiveRamp came from.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:26.390
All of our technology was
in a single colo facility

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:28.140
based in San Francisco.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:33.050
We have about 100 petabytes
of servers in our colocation

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:34.160
facility.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:38.010
And we were hitting this point
where we were scaling really,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.340
really quickly as a business.

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:43.970
We needed to be able to
expand to new markets,

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:46.160
expand our products
more quickly,

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:48.860
launch new products
faster, and just continue

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:49.820
to innovate and grow.

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:52.040
Because we're in a
very high growth phase.

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:54.620
And we were hitting
limitations with the fact

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:58.580
that we had all of our data
and all of our services

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:02.630
within a one colocation
facility, which was

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:03.890
getting difficult to manage.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:07.160
And we weren't able to order
servers fast enough in order

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:08.660
to keep up with our growth.

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:10.850
So we recognized
a couple of years

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:13.850
ago that we actually really
needed to move to the cloud.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:16.433
And then we went through a very
rigorous process of looking

00:21:16.433 --> 00:21:18.350
at what are the different
solutions out there,

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:22.910
what do we think our
needs are, and do

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:24.620
we want to go with
a hybrid solution,

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.780
do we want to go
all in on the cloud.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:33.300
And essentially, it came down
two decision-making factors.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:35.840
One was looking at what was
the technology available.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:37.490
And then one was
looking at what are

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:39.410
the costs associated with that.

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:41.690
And we did a pretty
extensive model,

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:43.670
where we were looking
very specifically

00:21:43.670 --> 00:21:46.400
at the kinds of
technologies that we would

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:48.740
use within each cloud provider.

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:51.230
How much scale we
would need for that.

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:53.270
Modeling what we
thought the price

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:56.210
was going to be based on
our various conversations

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:58.340
with different cloud providers.

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:00.380
Evaluating the
technologies for the scale

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:01.940
that we are operating at.

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:04.970
And while we have
many similarities

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:08.080
in terms of the scale we're
operating at to OpenX,

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:09.560
we have key differences.

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:13.525
We're not doing a lot
of real-time networking.

00:22:13.525 --> 00:22:15.650
We don't have the same kind
of real-time networking

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:16.460
challenges.

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:19.310
We're doing a lot of
big data processing

00:22:19.310 --> 00:22:23.640
on huge amounts of amounts
of data that were coming in.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.610
So the way our
product works is we

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:28.800
work with hundreds of brands,
take all of their data,

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:31.400
are processing that
data, and activating it

00:22:31.400 --> 00:22:34.100
across hundreds of
platforms, bringing data

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:37.310
back in from those platforms,
and giving that back

00:22:37.310 --> 00:22:40.070
to the brand or putting that
into different measurement

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:40.910
solutions.

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:43.400
So we're processing
just tons and tons

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:46.020
of data every single day.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:52.220
And so a lot of the
challenges that we have

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.750
are fairly different than
a lot of other companies

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:58.550
that are storing a lot of data
and processing a lot of data.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:01.190
For instance, when we thought
about moving to the cloud,

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:05.210
one of the challenges
we had is we're

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.050
basically a data
processing company,

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:10.650
so we don't have a lot of
data that's in cold storage.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:13.340
We have a lot of
data that is live

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:14.870
and it's moving
through a pipeline.

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:16.400
And so when we're
migrating, we're

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:18.390
thinking about what is
this going to look like.

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:20.180
We have to think about
how do we do this

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.070
without disrupting our clients.

00:23:22.070 --> 00:23:23.570
And so there are a
lot of challenges

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:27.082
that we had to go through in
thinking through this process.

00:23:27.082 --> 00:23:28.790
The key thing that we
were thinking about

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:31.020
is, how do we
expand our business?

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.780
How do we continue to grow?

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:34.820
And a key part of
what we're focused on

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:36.770
is we're a company
all about helping

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:38.180
people activate their data.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:42.920
We have 550 integrations
today across a broad range

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:44.580
of categories.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.100
And for us, we have
to think about,

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.210
how do we build these
integrations faster?

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:51.260
How do we improve the way
we're pushing and pulling

00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:54.620
data from all of these
different ecosystem players?

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:58.310
And how do we just continue to
offer more and more use cases

00:23:58.310 --> 00:23:59.460
to our clients.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.420
So all of this was part
of our thought process

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:04.640
as were thinking about
moving to the cloud

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:08.780
and being able to offer
products and innovate faster.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:11.420
As we also looked at what are
the growth factors for us.

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:13.040
There are a lot
of growth factors.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:15.330
Again, we're a very
high growth company.

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:17.330
Some of the ones that I
wanted to point out here

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:19.070
is international expansion.

00:24:19.070 --> 00:24:22.520
We really needed the ability
to expand internationally

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:23.790
as quickly as possible.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:28.310
And with all of the data privacy
regulation coming into effect,

00:24:28.310 --> 00:24:31.610
we had to think about, how do
we have more data available just

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:32.340
in region?

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.700
How do we create the right
permissions around it?

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:36.200
Other parts of our
growth are really

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.460
focused around data sharing.

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:40.580
So our Data Store
product enables companies

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:42.500
to monetize their products.

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:44.690
Our second party data
products enabled brands

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:48.530
to share the data with
each other in secure data

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:49.610
partnerships.

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:52.730
And these are products that we
had to think about, how are we

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:55.397
going to build, how are we going
to create the right permissions

00:24:55.397 --> 00:24:55.940
around this?

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:58.520
And all of this
factored into the way

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.630
we thought about picking
our cloud provider.

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:03.930
So why did we end
up choosing Google?

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.030
There are many reasons at play.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:10.320
As I talked about, it was both
a technology decision and a cost

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:11.310
decision.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:13.620
And when we started
doing the evaluation

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:15.820
on the technology itself
and we thought about,

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:18.900
hey, what's most important to
our clients, what it came down

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:21.030
to was security.

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.700
When we're talking
about data partnerships,

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:27.600
we're taking into our
system valuable data

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:30.330
from a very broad
swath of the ecosystem.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:32.920
And people expect that
data to be protected.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.770
They expect that
data to be safe.

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:38.100
And we evaluated the
security practices.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:40.110
And we have very rigorous
security practices

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:42.815
for ourselves in our
colocation facility.

00:25:42.815 --> 00:25:44.190
We had to think
about, how are we

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.590
going to port that
over to the cloud?

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.960
And we were really
impressed by the security

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:50.460
that Google had in place.

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:53.310
And we've actually been
able to up the game in terms

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:55.860
of our security
practices and unlock

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:58.140
a whole new set of use
cases for us, especially

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:00.490
as we get into more
regulated industries

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:03.730
and are opening up new use
cases by working with Google.

00:26:03.730 --> 00:26:06.870
The second thing for us is that
we have evolved very quickly,

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:09.130
but we are still a
10-year-old company.

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:12.360
So we had to do an immense
amount of infrastructure

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:13.650
modernization.

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.270
And we went through
a big process,

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:17.790
even before we started
migrating, of containerizing

00:26:17.790 --> 00:26:20.592
all of our code.

00:26:20.592 --> 00:26:22.050
One of the things
that we've really

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:23.905
appreciated in
partnering with Google

00:26:23.905 --> 00:26:25.530
is that they've been
able to provide us

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:29.670
professional services and other
advisory around how to actually

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:32.280
migrate our systems to the
cloud, which has actually

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.020
sped up the process for us.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:39.420
So we signed our GCP agreement
in July of last year.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:42.120
We are hoping to
be on track to be

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:45.610
fully migrated by the
end of August this year.

00:26:45.610 --> 00:26:48.640
So we've gone through
a pretty fast process

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.430
and we've had lots
of different pieces

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:51.513
that we've had to migrate.

00:26:51.513 --> 00:26:53.400
We have our core
systems here in the US.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:57.387
We have systems internationally
that are slightly different.

00:26:57.387 --> 00:26:59.220
And so we've had many
different environments

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:00.450
that we've had to move over.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:02.200
And as part of that,
really thought about,

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:04.125
how do we modernize
our infrastructure?

00:27:04.125 --> 00:27:06.000
The other thing that's
really important to us

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:08.610
is, like I said, we're a
big data processing company.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.370
So looking at the tools that
are available within Google

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:13.700
and recognizing that we
can be part of the road

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:17.670
map of developing those tools
to better suit our business

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.450
was a critical component.

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:21.030
And underlying all
of that, there's

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:22.590
the people that we worked with.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.990
When you're entering into a
relationship of this size,

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:29.430
and this is a substantial
investment for us financially,

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:32.070
it's going to help us
improve our gross margins.

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:34.920
We're thinking about how does
this improve our partnership

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:36.010
opportunities.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:38.530
This is very, very
fundamental to our business.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:41.760
And so we were looking for
someone we could really partner

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:44.490
with and someone who
would lean in with us

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.770
and really help us
innovate faster.

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:48.900
And know that if
we had an issue,

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:52.110
we could pick up the phone
and call someone and get help

00:27:52.110 --> 00:27:53.160
for what we needed to.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.950
And honestly, throughout
this migration process

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:58.590
that we've been
through, it keeps

00:27:58.590 --> 00:28:00.660
getting reinforced that
the people that we're

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:03.060
working with at Google
are just amazing.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.010
And the technical
expertise they're

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:08.610
able to bring to the table and
bring to our engineering team

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:10.552
has helped us migrate
really quickly.

00:28:10.552 --> 00:28:12.510
And I think it bodes
really well for the future

00:28:12.510 --> 00:28:16.120
as we continue to
think about innovation.

00:28:16.120 --> 00:28:18.730
The last part that I
think is really important,

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:22.720
and Paul mentioned this as
well, is our overall partnership

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:24.160
with Google.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:27.370
For us, we've had a very
long standing relationship

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:29.800
with the ad side of
the Google business.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:33.850
And we connect tons of data
into AdWords, into DoubleClick,

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:35.740
and into some of
the newer products

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:38.600
within the Google
Ads stack as well.

00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:41.198
And, as we were
thinking about who

00:28:41.198 --> 00:28:43.240
do we want to partner with
for the future, for us

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.450
it was an obvious decision
to partner with Google.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.600
Because it brings us closer
to the Google's business,

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:54.100
but also because we see so many
of our customers and partners

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:56.230
adopting solutions
on top of Google.

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:58.340
And when we're talking
about integrating data,

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:01.870
if we're all integrated on top
of the same technology stack,

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:04.010
it makes that integration
so much easier.

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:05.782
It makes the data
flow so much faster.

00:29:05.782 --> 00:29:07.240
And these are all
things that we're

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:09.755
considering as we're
looking to the future

00:29:09.755 --> 00:29:12.130
and making sure that we have
a partner who's really going

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:14.420
to help us grow the business.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:15.798
Thank you.

00:29:15.798 --> 00:29:19.291
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:20.290 --> 00:29:22.665
Now I'd like to introduce
Michael from Urban Airship.

00:29:28.922 --> 00:29:29.630
MIKE HERRICK: Hi.

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:34.090
Yes, I'm Mike Herrick.

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:37.120
I run technology
for Urban Airship.

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:40.120
And we're a little bit
ahead of these two companies

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:43.230
in terms of our journey.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:46.060
Ours started three years
ago, at least for me it did.

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:49.330
Three years ago here at Next,
where I met with Google.

00:29:49.330 --> 00:29:51.370
We had been evaluating them.

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:58.800
We had previously been 80% in
a colo facility and 20% on AWD.

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:04.300
And so our colo is very quickly
deteriorating underneath us.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:07.900
We were dealing with failing
hard drives, failing networks.

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.520
And it was time to re platform.

00:30:09.520 --> 00:30:13.090
And we were considering either
adding a couple of colos

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:17.450
and investing in more hardware
or fully moving to the cloud.

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:22.700
And thankfully, we made a great
choice and moved to Google.

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:26.710
It was a gigantic
undertaking, but we

00:30:26.710 --> 00:30:31.390
chose it just given all
the great technology fit.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.820
We used to run a
ton at each base

00:30:33.820 --> 00:30:36.490
and each base migrated
really well to Bigtable.

00:30:36.490 --> 00:30:39.010
And that was really
important to us.

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:40.360
The migration really went well.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:42.410
I'll tell you a
bit more about it.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:45.130
But it really helped
us manage cost.

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:49.623
It's really hard to manage costs
in a colo for a SaaS company.

00:30:49.623 --> 00:30:51.790
It really helped us make
our platform more scalable.

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:53.620
I'll show you some
stuff about that.

00:30:53.620 --> 00:30:57.730
And more reliable, which really
can't say enough about that

00:30:57.730 --> 00:31:00.760
in terms of just the quality
of life of our engineers.

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:04.870
And just the customer
satisfaction that we now enjoy.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:08.680
And also, now that we're
on the other side of it,

00:31:08.680 --> 00:31:11.080
we no longer are
doing crazy things

00:31:11.080 --> 00:31:14.830
like balancing Cassandra
rings, and HBase clusters,

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:16.510
and tuning Kafka.

00:31:16.510 --> 00:31:18.130
And so our engineers
are able now

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:22.930
to innovate much further up
the stack, which is great.

00:31:22.930 --> 00:31:26.200
So just briefly a little
bit about Urban Airship.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:28.540
Urban Airship is a customer
engagement platform.

00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:33.430
So we focus primarily on things
like retention and growth

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:34.310
for our customers.

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:36.190
We help a little bit on
the acquisition side.

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:40.730
But mostly focused
on those two things.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.490
You may know us.

00:31:42.490 --> 00:31:45.670
We were the original third
party push notification company,

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:49.690
but we've expanded dramatically
and create many different types

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:51.070
of experiences these days.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:55.720
We expanded into various
in-app experiences.

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:58.690
We work with Google and Apple
on things like Google Pay,

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:00.400
so mobile wallets.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.010
We power a lot of your
boarding passes, probably,

00:32:03.010 --> 00:32:07.510
if you flew some of the
airlines that we support.

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:12.280
We do things on the web,
email, SMS, some other channels

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.090
as well.

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:16.170
And we operate at a
pretty great scale.

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:19.540
Ad tech is the only thing
that ever makes me feel small.

00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:25.630
But we send 90 billion messages
a month across these channels

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:31.160
and regularly exceed
500,000 messages a second.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:32.300
And our customer base.

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.128
We're pretty much
geared for enterprise,

00:32:34.128 --> 00:32:35.920
although we have
customers large and small.

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:41.430
And we have 25% of the
Fortune 100 are our customers.

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:45.870
Really briefly, this is
a look at our platform.

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.690
We talked a bit about channels.

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:50.050
We support many of them.

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:51.743
We also have this
open channel API

00:32:51.743 --> 00:32:53.160
that a number of
our customers use

00:32:53.160 --> 00:32:58.290
to reach things like
over the top networks

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:00.940
that they can't reach
through any other way.

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.340
And so that's nice technology.

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:05.440
And we've been investing heavily
in our orchestration layer.

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:09.090
So that's all sorts of things
around targeting, segmentation,

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:12.030
automation, and orchestration
across channels.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:13.560
Optimizing them.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:17.010
And then all these
experiences that we

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:20.910
help our customers create send
back a lot of first party data

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:21.870
to Urban Airship.

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:24.000
And we help them
make use of that

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:27.870
by providing rich user
profiles, performance analytics

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:32.880
on that data, machine learning,
and AI capabilities, things

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:36.270
like predictive churn,
same time optimization.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:39.480
And then we have some technology
to stream this data that we

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:42.720
process out to our customers.

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:45.540
Other aspects of their
marketing technology stack.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:48.450
Things like BI tools.

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:51.090
Some of our customers use it
to send it to ad networks.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:53.940
That sort of thing.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:57.170
So a couple examples of
experiences that we provide.

00:33:57.170 --> 00:33:58.890
We work across verticals.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:04.070
So travel, retail,
media, financial.

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:08.050
I mentioned a lot of airlines.

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:09.949
Our point of view is
the best marketing

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:11.600
isn't really marketing at all.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:13.670
It's these useful
types of experiences,

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:19.719
where you can provide value and
experiences that users value.

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:23.630
So in travel, things like, hey,
your seat has been upgraded.

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:26.330
In retail, your
package has shipped.

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:29.360
In media, a story that you're
interested in because you're

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:30.830
following a journalist.

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:33.600
Financially, here's
your account balance.

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:36.210
So we do all sorts
of things like that.

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.449
Most recently, just last
night, I put this in there

00:34:38.449 --> 00:34:39.889
because it was timely.

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:41.730
The NCA is a customer.

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:46.340
And so we've powered all of
their March Madness messaging.

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:50.120
So they do a great job with
anything from score alerts,

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.730
to sending videos through
rich notifications,

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.900
to even last night after the
game, that kind of famous one

00:34:56.900 --> 00:34:59.870
shining moment
video was sent out.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:01.440
So we were busy last night.

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:05.730
Another quick story.

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:08.680
So last year for
the World Cup, soon

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:12.790
after we finished our
migration to Google Cloud,

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:15.610
we had a bunch of customers
that were really excited

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:17.980
about the World Cup.

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:20.890
Particular one
football really wanted

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:23.620
to make sure that their
notifications arrived

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:24.820
as quickly as possible.

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:28.360
So we worked closely with
them on some technology

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:30.080
that we call Boost.

00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:32.020
And this is just a graph.

00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:34.675
This was the end of the
World Cup last year.

00:35:34.675 --> 00:35:38.960
And we were sending
400,000 notifications

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:40.750
a second for minutes.

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:42.380
That's across our customer base.

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:46.930
We power tons of
media and sports apps.

00:35:46.930 --> 00:35:51.940
Since then, we've
exceeded this milestone

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:57.260
and regularly exceed 500,000
notifications a second.

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:00.850
But just to lift
the hood up a bit.

00:36:00.850 --> 00:36:02.980
I won't delve into
too much detail.

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:05.200
But this is a look at
one of our subsystems.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:10.000
We call it the CDP, the
Core Delivery Platform.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.090
So the scale that
we operate means

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:15.340
that we have to
do a lot of things

00:36:15.340 --> 00:36:17.320
that maybe we wish
we wouldn't have

00:36:17.320 --> 00:36:20.560
to in terms of microservices
and really kind of breaking

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:22.220
some of these components apart.

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:25.300
So we have what we call
a distributed query

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:29.740
planner, which deals with
a lot of these subsystems.

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:31.870
And it leverages a bunch
of Google technology,

00:36:31.870 --> 00:36:33.650
like Bigtable.

00:36:33.650 --> 00:36:37.000
We leverage a ton of
Cloud Pub/Sub and also

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.710
Compute Engine.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.530
But by leveraging
Google technology,

00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:45.220
again, compared
back to our colo,

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:48.940
we were able to easily add
capacity for the World Cup.

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:51.050
We can scale it up and down.

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:53.230
And we also were
able to leverage

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:57.190
some of our own innovation
by doing a bunch of software

00:36:57.190 --> 00:37:00.160
optimization, doing a lot
of parallel processing,

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:03.670
some advanced queuing of some
of these priority requests.

00:37:03.670 --> 00:37:06.280
And so by doing that, we
were able to really meet

00:37:06.280 --> 00:37:07.630
our customers' needs.

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:11.170
I think if we hadn't completed
our move to Google Cloud,

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:14.030
just because the World Cup
was so big for our business,

00:37:14.030 --> 00:37:16.220
it probably wouldn't
have gone nearly as well.

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:19.690
So that was great.

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:25.150
So I mentioned that we started
our journey 80% on colo,

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:27.340
20% on Amazon.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:30.220
We're expanding our
footprint more globally now.

00:37:30.220 --> 00:37:32.330
So we're moving into
additional regions.

00:37:32.330 --> 00:37:34.330
And so it just made sense
for us to consolidate.

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:36.490
We've had somewhat
success with Google.

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:37.870
Our engineers really love it.

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:40.660
And so we've just
collapsed the stack.

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:42.760
This is one of the
projects that our team

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:45.550
is working on right now.

00:37:45.550 --> 00:37:47.810
We have business
intelligence capabilities.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:50.470
So it's really fine
grained user-level data

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:51.470
at the event level.

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:55.360
So just tons and tons of data.

00:37:55.360 --> 00:38:00.492
And it's currently
running on Redshift

00:38:00.492 --> 00:38:01.950
and has been for
a couple of years.

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:03.670
And that's worked OK.

00:38:03.670 --> 00:38:06.010
But our customers
love this product.

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:07.510
They want to see
better performance.

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:09.250
They want fresher data.

00:38:09.250 --> 00:38:12.100
And so Bigtable and
BigQuery are really

00:38:12.100 --> 00:38:15.110
helping us deliver on
that for our customers.

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:18.220
And so currently we're
loading this data daily

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:19.390
just because it's so much.

00:38:19.390 --> 00:38:21.640
And just given some of the
economics that at least

00:38:21.640 --> 00:38:23.195
we experience with Redshift.

00:38:23.195 --> 00:38:24.820
But we're going to
be able to move that

00:38:24.820 --> 00:38:26.783
to minutes instead of per day.

00:38:26.783 --> 00:38:29.200
So our customers, as they've
been hearing about this news,

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:31.630
are pretty excited.

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:35.630
And also our storage is
now separated from compute.

00:38:35.630 --> 00:38:37.600
If you know anything
about Redshift,

00:38:37.600 --> 00:38:39.110
this will make sense to you.

00:38:39.110 --> 00:38:43.270
So we can say goodbye to nodes
in reserved instances, which

00:38:43.270 --> 00:38:46.430
is great for our cost.

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:49.750
And next, just from a
complexity standpoin--

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:52.540
some of this is just the
way we built the system.

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:54.790
We're doing a lot of software
updates to this as well.

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:58.780
But with this new
version of the system,

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:01.840
we're moving from
some complicated ETL

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:05.830
that we kind of crafted from
canisius and microservices

00:39:05.830 --> 00:39:08.120
to one single dataflow job.

00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:12.430
So just really dramatically
simplifies the system.

00:39:12.430 --> 00:39:15.612
And then just our maintenance
overhead is reduced.

00:39:15.612 --> 00:39:17.320
We now have to deal
with vacuums anymore,

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:21.520
which has been
less than awesome.

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:23.380
And then BigQuery
cluster tables really

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:25.650
enable efficient queries.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:28.330
And for some of the queries,
given the complexity of data,

00:39:28.330 --> 00:39:30.600
have to do with a lot of
scanning of the entire data

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:33.810
set, which is really slow.

00:39:33.810 --> 00:39:35.670
So that's great.

00:39:35.670 --> 00:39:39.000
And then, as we
look to the future,

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:41.670
we have big aspirations
for our company

00:39:41.670 --> 00:39:45.180
and creating more and more
capabilities in our customer

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:47.690
engagement platform.

00:39:47.690 --> 00:39:52.507
And so we're seeking to leverage
more and more of Google Cloud.

00:39:52.507 --> 00:39:54.590
Right now all of our machine
learning capabilities

00:39:54.590 --> 00:39:56.000
are built in-house.

00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:57.950
And we're experimenting
with leveraging

00:39:57.950 --> 00:39:59.480
some of the Google's stuff.

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:02.030
Our job is to innovate
further up the stack

00:40:02.030 --> 00:40:06.320
and not bother with
things like managing

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:07.770
each base in hard drives.

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:09.450
So anyways, that's my story.

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:10.430
Thank you.

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:14.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

